			<h2 id="questions">23. Questions people ask</h2>
			<div class="panel">
				<div class="faq">
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Can I use Teachouse for a school account?</summary>
						<p>
							Yes, if you are allowed to act for the school and to sell what you publish. Some
							employment agreements give the school rights in resources made for work, so check
							yours. When you use Teachouse for a school or business, you agree to these terms for
							it, and section 17 applies to it as a business.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Can two people use one account?</summary>
						<p>
							Yes. You can both use the same Teachouse account and each install the app on your own
							device. The account holder is responsible for what happens in it.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>What happens to my listings if I cancel?</summary>
						<p>
							Nothing on the marketplace. Your plan runs to the end of the period you paid for, then
							you move to {freeName}. Live listings stay live, your catalogue and moves stay where
							they are, and if you are over a {freeName} limit you keep everything but can't add
							more.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>What happens if I close my account?</summary>
						<p>
							We delete your account and catalogue as the Privacy Policy describes. Your listings stay
							on each marketplace, and files you imported stay on your devices. Export your
							catalogue first if you want a copy.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Do you see my passwords?</summary>
						<p>
							No. You sign in to a marketplace with no official connection inside the Teachouse app
							on your own device. Your password goes to the marketplace, never to us, and the sign-in
							stays on that device. Your Teachouse password is stored only as a one-way hash, which
							we can't read. If you ask us for help, an administrator may view your account as you
							see it, and every view is recorded.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Do you keep my files?</summary>
						<p>
							Not the files you import: they stay on your devices and pass through without being
							kept. Files you upload to Teachouse yourself are stored, encrypted, until you delete
							them. Deleting a resource in Teachouse doesn't delete your own copy of the file.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Do you sell my resources or take a share of my sales?</summary>
						<p>
							No. You sell on the marketplaces, and they pay you. We only charge for plans and Move
							Packs.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Could a marketplace object to me using Teachouse?</summary>
						<p>
							It could. Teachouse only acts in your own shop, on your own resources, when you ask,
							but each marketplace decides what it allows. Read its terms, and if a marketplace
							asks you to stop using a tool, tell us.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>I published and it failed. Did I lose a move?</summary>
						<p>
							A move only counts when the listing reaches the marketplace. If it never arrived, it
							costs nothing.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>Can I get a refund?</summary>
						<p>
							Yes, for a Move Pack you haven't used, within 14 days of buying it, and for the unused
							whole months of a yearly plan, less one month's fee. A monthly plan isn't refunded once
							its month has started; cancelling stops the next renewal.
							<a class="link" href="#refunds">Section 11</a> has the details.
						</p>
					</details>
					<details class="qa">
						<summary>I'm not in New Zealand. Can I still use Teachouse?</summary>
						<p>
							Yes. Teachouse is run from New Zealand, prices are in US dollars, and these terms are
							governed by New Zealand law wherever you are.
						</p>
					</details>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
